window.SS = window.SS || {};
Object.assign(window.SS, {"chapters":[{"id":1,"emoji":"🧠","color":"#6C5CE7","title":"Brain User Manual","sub":"How learning happens","intro":"Studying well is a skill you can learn. Once you know how your brain takes in and stores information, you'll see why some methods work so well and others just eat up time.","lessons":[{"id":"1-1","emoji":"🎯","title":"Why learn study skills?","points":["Study skills aren't just for school. You'll use them for sports, instruments, languages and everything you'll learn at work later on.","With good methods you remember <b>more in the same time</b>, get less tired, and studying feels less painful.","Research is closer to ‘<b>people who know the methods study well</b>’ than to ‘you need to be smart to study well’.","The goal of this app: become a <b>self-directed learner</b> who plans → acts → checks on their own!"],"tip":"Think of one subject you studied today and write one line about ‘how’ you studied it. Noticing your methods is the first step.","quiz":[{"q":"Which best describes study skills?","o":["Only people born with them can have them","They're needed only during exam season","A lifelong skill anyone can improve with practice","A teacher can do them for you"],"a":2,"e":"Study skills are learned and built through practice — and you'll use them for whatever you learn in life."}]},{"id":"1-2","emoji":"⚡","title":"How the Brain Makes Memories","points":["New information first enters your <b>working memory</b> (a mental notepad). It holds only a few things at once, so it overflows quickly.","When you understand the meaning, connect it to what you know, and <b>retrieve it many times</b>, it moves firmly into <b>long-term memory</b>.","Connections between brain cells get stronger the more you use them and weaker when you don't. ‘Recalling’ is exactly the exercise that strengthens them.","While you sleep, your brain sorts and stores what you learned during the day. That's why all-nighters backfire."],"fact":{"title":"Science fact-check: ‘left-brain vs right-brain’?","text":"The idea that there are logical ‘left-brain’ people and creative ‘right-brain’ people is a widespread myth. In reality, both sides of the brain work together in almost everything. What matters isn't which side, but <b>how actively you think</b>."},"game":"digitspan","quiz":[{"q":"Which is true about working memory?","o":["Its capacity is unlimited","It can hold only a little at a time","It grows when you sleep","Once something enters, it stays forever"],"a":1,"e":"Working memory is small. That's why you need strategies to group information (chunking) and move it into long-term memory."},{"q":"What does science say about ‘left-brain/right-brain people’?","o":["It's true, so study according to your type","Both sides of the brain work together in most activities","Right-brain people can't do math","Left-brain people can't draw"],"a":1,"e":"Left/right-brain types are a myth with weak support in brain science."}]},{"id":"1-3","emoji":"🪜","title":"3 Steps of Effective Study","points":["<b>① Understand</b> — before memorizing, be able to explain ‘why’.","<b>② See it from many angles</b> — complete the big picture with examples, pictures and links to other units.","<b>③ Remember and use it</b> — close the book, recall, and apply it to problems. That's when it truly becomes yours.","Many students stop at ① (just reading) or walk into the exam without ③. Build the habit of going through all three steps."],"tip":"Close the book and say out loud: ‘How would I explain what I just read to a friend in 30 seconds?’ Where you get stuck is exactly what to review.","quiz":[{"q":"Which activity is ‘③ Remember and use it’?","o":["Reading the textbook once more","Highlighting important sentences","Closing the book and writing the key points on a blank page","Neatly copying your notes again"],"a":2,"e":"‘Retrieval’ — recalling onto a blank page — makes memories strongest."}]},{"id":"1-4","emoji":"🏃","title":"Active vs Passive Learning","points":["<b>Passive learning</b>: copying long paragraphs, rewriting notes neatly, just reading many times. It feels like hard work, but little sticks.","<b>Active learning</b>: making questions, drawing mind maps, teaching a friend, squeezing a paragraph into one sentence and then into 3 hashtags, arguing the opposite side.","The key difference: does your brain <b>create something itself</b>? What you create stays longer (the generation effect).","When you finish studying, write <b>‘3 new things I learned + 1 thing I still wonder about’</b>. A 30-second active-learning wrap-up."],"sort":{"title":"Sorting game: active or passive?","buckets":["Active learning","Passive learning"],"items":[["Copy a textbook paragraph word for word",1],["Explain what you learned to a younger sibling",0],["Highlight almost every line",1],["Squeeze a unit into 3 hashtags",0],["Neatly rewrite your notes",1],["Close the book and make your own problems to solve",0],["Apply a teacher's feedback to this assignment",0],["Play an explanation video like background music",1]]},"quiz":[{"q":"Which is active learning?","o":["Rewriting notes neatly","Reading the same page five times","Making and solving a quiz on what you learned","Highlighting"],"a":2,"e":"Making and answering your own questions is classic active learning."}]},{"id":"1-5","emoji":"🎨","title":"Learning Styles: Real or Not?","points":["Everyone has <b>preferred ways</b> to learn — pictures, talk, text or movement.","But the claim that ‘visual learners improve only with pictures and auditory learners only by listening’ hasn't been confirmed by experiments.","A better way: choose <b>the method that fits the content</b> (maps as pictures, pronunciation as sound) and <b>mix several methods</b>. Using words + pictures together (‘dual coding’) is a great example.","Knowing your preferences helps make studying <b>fun</b>. Just don't lock yourself into one style!"],"fact":{"title":"Science fact-check: teaching to learning styles","text":"Psychologist Pashler and colleagues (2008) reviewed the idea that ‘teaching matched to learning styles works better’, but found almost no support in properly designed experiments. So this app's check isn't a ‘type test’ — it's a tool to find <b>what you enjoy + what to try more</b>."},"game":"styles","quiz":[{"q":"Which is closest to the research on learning styles?","o":["You must use only methods that match your type","Preferences exist, but there's little evidence that type-matched learning raises grades","Learning styles never change in life","Auditory learners shouldn't read books"],"a":1,"e":"Preferences exist, but ‘matched-style learning’ hasn't been proven effective. Mixing methods is better."}]}]},{"id":2,"emoji":"🔥","color":"#E84393","title":"Mind Engine","sub":"Motivation · self-direction · mindset","intro":"What drives studying is ultimately your mind. Find your own reasons, take the wheel yourself, and build the mental muscle to turn failure into information.","lessons":[{"id":"2-1","emoji":"🔥","title":"Building Motivation","points":["Motivation differs from person to person. The starting point is finding <b>your own reason</b> for ‘why learn this?’","Set <b>long-term goals</b> (dreams, career) and <b>short-term goals</b> (today, this week) together. Each small goal you tick off adds to your sense of achievement.","When you reach a goal, give yourself a <b>small reward</b> — a favorite snack, a walk, 15 minutes of gaming.","A hard problem isn't a threat — it's a <b>challenge</b>. Problems you got stuck on and then solved are the ones you remember longest.","When your ‘why’ feels weak, write <b>a one-line letter to your future self</b>. What will you thank yourself for a year from now?"],"tip":"Use the ‘SMART Goal Maker’ in the Toolbox to set one goal for this week.","toolLink":"goal","quiz":[{"q":"Which does NOT help keep motivation?","o":["Breaking a big goal into small steps","Giving a small reward when you succeed","Constantly comparing yourself to others and self-blaming","Writing goals where you can see them"],"a":2,"e":"Comparison and self-blame eat away at motivation. Compare yourself with yesterday's you."}]},{"id":"2-2","emoji":"🧑‍✈️","title":"Be the Pilot of Your Learning","points":["Teachers show the direction, but <b>asking and finding out is your job</b>.","First write down your <b>strengths</b> (asking friends is fine). Strengths are fuel for confidence.","Mistakes aren't defeats — they're <b>information</b>. Analyze ‘why did I get it wrong?’ and your next action appears.","Everyone does ‘avoidance activities’. The fix is simple — <b>start the first step right now</b>. Just 5 minutes!","Self-directed learners don't just do what they're told; they get curious and dig deeper on their own."],"tip":"The ‘5-minute rule’: do the study you don't want to do for just 5 minutes. Once you start, you'll usually keep going.","quiz":[{"q":"What's the simplest way to beat procrastination?","o":["Wait until you feel motivated","Make the first step tiny and start right away","Cram it all right before the deadline","Keep your to-do list only in your head"],"a":1,"e":"Action creates motivation. Start small, start now."}]},{"id":"2-3","emoji":"🌱","title":"Growth Mindset","points":["<b>Fixed mindset</b>: ‘I'm just smart/not smart.’ — students who think they're smart don't try, and those who think they're not give up.","<b>Growth mindset</b>: ‘Ability grows with effort and strategy.’ — difficulties are part of learning.","The magic word <b>‘yet’</b>: ‘I'm bad at math’ → ‘I can't solve this type <b>yet</b>’.","Effort alone isn't enough. Skills grow with <b>effort + good strategies + asking for help</b>."],"fact":{"title":"Science fact-check","text":"Analyses combining growth-mindset studies found the average effect was smaller than expected. But some results show it helps students who are struggling. So use it <b>together with concrete study strategies</b>, not just as a belief."},"game":"mindset","quiz":[{"q":"Which is a growth-mindset statement?","o":["I'm just not a language person","I'm a genius, so I don't need to study","This grammar still confuses me, but making more example sentences will help","It won't work anyway"],"a":2,"e":"‘Yet’ + a concrete strategy = growth mindset."}]},{"id":"2-4","emoji":"🪞","title":"Letting Go of Perfectionism","points":["Wanting everything perfect often <b>stops you from starting</b>.","The goal isn't ‘a masterpiece’ but <b>‘a good-enough finished draft’</b>. You can only fix what's finished.","Write first drafts <b>freely and fast</b> (freewriting). Polishing comes later.","With several assignments, <b>start all of them a little</b> instead of perfecting one.","Mistakes are part of learning. Don't be too hard on yourself."],"quiz":[{"q":"A good strategy for perfectionism?","o":["Don't write until the first sentence is perfect","Draft fast, fix later","Perfect one assignment and give up the rest","Don't submit for fear of mistakes"],"a":1,"e":"‘Finish first, improve later’ is the key to escaping perfectionism."}]}]},{"id":3,"emoji":"⏱️","color":"#00B894","title":"Time & Focus","sub":"Space · focus · planning","intro":"The same hour can feel like 10 minutes or 2 hours depending on your environment and plan. Learn to set up your space, protect your focus and design your week.","lessons":[{"id":"3-1","emoji":"🪑","title":"Your Own Study Space","points":["Quiet, bright and tidy is enough. A tidy space makes you want to sit down again.","Some people like one spot; others feel fresh changing places. Find <b>the routine that suits you</b>.","Put your timetable and deadlines on the wall and mark tasks with sticky notes.","Tidy your desk when you finish! Organizing your computer desktop and files into subject folders saves search time too.","Leaving <b>your phone in another room</b> is the most powerful focus device there is."],"fact":{"title":"Research in one line","text":"In some studies, test scores on attention tasks dropped just because a phone was ‘lying on the desk’. <b>Out of sight</b> beats silent mode."},"quiz":[{"q":"Where's the best place to put your phone for focus?","o":["On the desk, screen up","On the desk, face down","In your pocket","In another room"],"a":3,"e":"The less visible it is, the less it steals your attention."}]},{"id":"3-2","emoji":"🎧","title":"The Secret of Focus","points":["<b>4-point focus check</b>: ① only this subject on the desk ② devices far away ③ one line stating this session's goal ④ set your end time and break time.","When your mind wanders, don't scold yourself — <b>gently bring it back</b>. Focus grows with practice.","<b>Multitasking is an illusion</b>. The brain just ‘switches’ quickly, and each switch costs efficiency. Studying with the TV on leaves both half-done.","<b>Chunking</b>: split work into blocks like ‘30 min: write conclusion’ and do only that in that time.","Being totally absorbed is called <b>flow</b>. It tends to come with tasks that are neither too easy nor too hard."],"tip":"Try one round of 25 min focus + 5 min break with the ‘Focus Timer’ in the Toolbox.","toolLink":"timer","quiz":[{"q":"Which is true about multitasking?","o":["Doing two things at once doubles efficiency","The brain loses efficiency every time it switches","TV helps you concentrate","Checking social media while studying is the same as resting"],"a":1,"e":"Multitasking is fast switching, and switching costs lower efficiency."}]},{"id":"3-3","emoji":"⏰","title":"Time Management & Priorities","points":["Write tasks as a <b>list</b> with the most important on top! Enjoy the satisfaction of ticking them off.","<b>A/B/C priorities</b>: A = must do today, B = should do, C = when there's time.","Besides the teacher's deadline, set <b>‘your own deadline’</b> a few days earlier. The gap becomes your time to improve.","For big assignments, place steps <b>backward from the deadline</b> and add a <b>buffer</b> in case you get sick.","A study-only plan won't last. Include friends, exercise and hobbies to keep a <b>balance</b>."],"tip":"Try the ‘Priority To-Do’ and ‘Backward Plan’ tools in the Toolbox.","toolLink":"todo","quiz":[{"q":"A good way to plan a big assignment?","o":["Do it all the day before the deadline","Work backward from the deadline to date each step","Do easy parts first in any order","Go with your mood, no plan"],"a":1,"e":"A backward plan + buffer time is the key to meeting deadlines."}]},{"id":"3-4","emoji":"🗓️","title":"Making & Keeping a Timetable","points":["First, record <b>how you actually spend time</b> on weekdays and weekends. You may have more free time than you think.","Put in class, sleep, exercise and meals first, then place study in the gaps. You may need to cut a little gaming or video time.","Give each activity a <b>color</b> and you'll see the balance at a glance.","A plan starts as a guess. Falling behind isn't failure — just <b>re-plan</b>. 10-minute check every Sunday!","Once a semester, draw <b>‘my learning map’</b>. Connect favorite subjects, interests and things you want to try, and you'll spot hints about your future path."],"tip":"See your time with your own eyes using the ‘Weekly Timetable’ and ‘24-Hour Pie Chart’ in the Toolbox.","toolLink":"week","quiz":[{"q":"Best response when your timetable doesn't go as planned?","o":["Throw away the timetable","Re-plan the rest realistically","Cut sleep to make it fit no matter what","Start over next month"],"a":1,"e":"Plans are meant to be revised. Check and adjust regularly."}]},{"id":"3-5","emoji":"💻","title":"Organizing Your Digital Study Space","points":["Create a <b>folder structure</b> by subject and set a file-naming rule. e.g. <code>science_photosynthesis_notes_v2</code>","<b>Back up regularly</b> (cloud + USB).","Typing speed and basic document and spreadsheet skills save time in every subject.","Set deadline reminders in a calendar or to-do app.","<b>3-2-1 backup</b>: 3 copies, 2 types of storage, 1 in a different place (cloud).","Check in advance that you have the <b>software</b> an assignment needs. There are often free alternatives."],"quiz":[{"q":"A good way to manage files?","o":["Everything on the desktop","Subject folders + naming rule + backup","Name files ‘New Document (1)’","Backups aren't needed"],"a":1,"e":"Organized folders and backups protect your time and work."},{"q":"In ‘3-2-1 backup’, what does the 1 mean?","o":["Save only 1 file","Keep 1 copy in a different place (e.g. cloud)","Once a week","One USB stick"],"a":1,"e":"Keep one copy elsewhere so you're safe even if one place fails."}]}]},{"id":4,"emoji":"📥","color":"#0984E3","title":"Input Skills","sub":"Reading · listening · searching · notes","intro":"Good studying needs good ingredients. These are the skills of reading smartly, listening well, choosing reliable information and recording it in your own words — plus studying safely and honestly online.","lessons":[{"id":"4-1","emoji":"👓","title":"Reading Smartly","points":["<b>Skimming</b>: get the overall flow from titles, headings and each paragraph's first sentence.","<b>Scanning</b>: quickly find only the words, numbers or names you need.","<b>Close reading</b>: read important parts slowly, asking questions.","Bonus strategy <b>SQ3R</b>: Survey → Question → Read → Recite → Review.","Real good readers <b>change their reading speed</b> to fit their purpose."],"game":"readmode","quiz":[{"q":"Which reading do you use to quickly find where ‘photosynthesis’ appears in your textbook?","o":["Close reading","Scanning","Reading aloud","Repeated reading"],"a":1,"e":"Scanning is efficient for finding specific information."},{"q":"What do you do in SQ3R's ‘R (Recite)’ step?","o":["Close the book and say what you read from memory","Find another book","Highlighting","Look only at the table of contents"],"a":0,"e":"Recite is retrieval practice. It makes memories solid."}]},{"id":"4-2","emoji":"👂","title":"Listening & Learning Together","points":["<b>Active listening</b>: skim the last lesson before class → note key words during class → ask when you don't understand → 5-minute review after class.","<b>Abbreviations</b> make notes faster. e.g. → (result), ∵ (because), e.g. (example), ★ (important).","In group work, split <b>roles</b>: leader, note-taker, researcher, timekeeper, presenter.","Keeping promises (responsibility), giving way (compromise) and respecting other views are the heart of teamwork.","Run projects in this order: <b>goal → roles → schedule → check-in → wrap-up</b>."],"quiz":[{"q":"How do you run a group project well?","o":["One person does everything","Set roles and a schedule and check in midway","Meet the day before the deadline","Everyone does whatever"],"a":1,"e":"Roles, schedule and check-ins are the 3 keys to team project success."}]},{"id":"4-3","emoji":"📝","title":"Taking Notes","points":["Don't write everything — only <b>key words and key sentences</b>.","Whenever possible, write <b>in your own words</b>. Rewording deepens understanding and prevents plagiarism.","For anything from a source, note the <b>reference (author, title, year, page/URL)</b> right away.","Note styles: standard linear, <b>split-page (Cornell)</b>, mind map. Choose by subject.","Notes aren't ‘storage’ — they're <b>material for testing yourself later</b>."],"tip":"Summarize one of today's lessons with the ‘Cornell Notes’ template in the Toolbox.","toolLink":"cornell","quiz":[{"q":"A good note-taking habit?","o":["Write down every word the teacher says","Key words in your own words, with sources noted","Use 10 colors of pens","Take notes and never look at them again"],"a":1,"e":"Key words + your own words + sources = good notes."}]},{"id":"4-4","emoji":"📚","title":"Finding Information & Using the Library","points":["The internet is convenient, but <b>good research often starts in the library</b>. Librarians are the best search helpers.","Learn to use the library's <b>online catalog</b> and find books on the shelf by call number. Books on similar topics sit together.","Know the rules for loan limits, periods, holds, renewals and late fees so you don't miss books you need.","With a long reading list, check the <b>contents, index and introduction</b> first and read only the parts you need."],"quiz":[{"q":"A good way to find more library books on the same topic?","o":["Pick books with pretty covers","Browse the shelves around the book you found (same call number)","Wander any floor","Pick the thickest book"],"a":1,"e":"Books with the same or nearby call numbers cover similar topics."}]},{"id":"4-5","emoji":"⚖️","title":"Evaluating Information","points":["<b>Who</b> wrote it? An expert or organization, or anonymous?","<b>When</b> was it written? Does the topic need up-to-date information?","<b>Fact or opinion?</b> What can be checked is fact; ‘should / is the best’ is opinion.","Is there <b>evidence</b>? Are sources, data or research given?","<b>Compare with other sources</b> on the same topic. Agreement across several places raises reliability."],"sort":{"title":"Sorting game: fact or opinion?","buckets":["Fact","Opinion"],"items":[["At 1 atmosphere, water boils at 100°C",0],["Summer is the best season",1],["The capital of Japan is Tokyo",0],["This movie is the best film of the year",1],["The Earth orbits the Sun",0],["Students should wear school uniforms",1],["This book was published in 2016",0],["Math is the most boring subject",1]]},"quiz":[{"q":"Which is an ‘opinion’?","o":["The first Moon landing was in 1969","Cats are mammals","Spanish is the most beautiful language in the world","Light travels faster than sound"],"a":2,"e":"‘The most beautiful’ is a personal judgment, so it's an opinion."}]},{"id":"4-6","emoji":"🔍","title":"Becoming a Search Pro","points":["Tell <b>primary sources</b> (originals, official statistics, papers) from <b>secondary sources</b> (commentary, summaries, blogs).","Search operators: <code>\"exact phrase\"</code> · <code>site:gov</code> (one site only) · <code>-excluded</code> · <code>filetype:pdf</code>.","Find academic material with scholarly search (e.g. Google Scholar), and public data on government or institution sites.","Organize useful sites in <b>bookmark folders</b>, and record the address, title and date of sources you use."],"game":"search","quiz":[{"q":"Which query finds ‘teen sleep’ material only on government sites?","o":["teen sleep government","teen sleep site:gov","\"government\" sleep","-teen sleep"],"a":1,"e":"The site: operator searches only within a specific domain."}]},{"id":"4-7","emoji":"🧾","title":"Avoiding Plagiarism & Using AI Well","points":["<b>Plagiarism</b>: using someone else's words or ideas as your own without credit. Even one sentence counts.","<b>Direct quotes</b> need quotation marks + a source; <b>paraphrasing</b> needs a source too.","How to paraphrase: cover the original → write in your own words → compare and reword anything too similar.","<b>AI rules</b>: it's a great ‘tutor’ to ask for explanations. But if it writes your assignment, your skills won't grow — and AI also gives convincing wrong answers. Check your school's rules and always verify facts with other sources."],"sort":{"title":"Judging game: is it OK?","buckets":["OK","Plagiarism/cheating"],"items":[["Quoted one sentence with quotation marks and a source",0],["Copied a blog paragraph and changed a few words",1],["Summarized in my own words and cited the source",0],["Submitted an essay written by AI under my name",1],["Got a concept explained by AI, then rewrote it in my own words",0],["Copied a friend's report as is",1]]},"quiz":[{"q":"When must you cite a source?","o":["Only for direct quotes","Only for paraphrasing","For both direct quotes and paraphrasing","Every time, even for common knowledge"],"a":2,"e":"If you borrowed someone's idea, cite it even if you changed the wording."}]},{"id":"4-8","emoji":"🛡️","title":"Social Media, Learning Platforms & Online Safety","points":["Social media helps study groups communicate, but <b>turn notifications off while studying</b>.","Check your school's online learning platform (LMS) regularly for assignments, notices and materials.","<b>Passwords</b>: long (12+ characters), different for every site, with 2-step verification on.","Don't open unknown attachments or links (phishing). Keep antivirus and security updates on.","Never post personal info (address, school, phone number) publicly."],"game":"password","quiz":[{"q":"The safest password habit?","o":["Same password for every site","Use your birthday","Long, unique passwords + 2-step verification","Write it on a sticky note on your monitor"],"a":2,"e":"Length + uniqueness + 2-step verification are the basics."}]},{"id":"4-9","emoji":"🎓","title":"A Sea of Online Courses","points":["<b>Free online courses</b> let you learn beyond school: Khan Academy, Coursera, edX and more.","Just ‘watching’ videos is passive learning. <b>Pause → predict → summarize → solve problems</b>.","Doing <b>one video properly at 1×</b> beats watching lots at 1.5×.","The secret to finishing a course: same time every week + a study buddy + small goals."],"quiz":[{"q":"How do you watch online lessons actively?","o":["Play at 2× and do something else","Pause now and then to summarize and predict","Read only the subtitles","Just download them"],"a":1,"e":"The moment you pause and think is the moment learning happens."}]}]},{"id":5,"emoji":"🔁","color":"#E58E26","title":"Memory Skills","sub":"Review · cards · mnemonics","intro":"Review isn't something you do right before a test — you build it a little at a time all year. Learn how memories last and use review tools (cards, mind maps, mnemonics).","lessons":[{"id":"5-1","emoji":"🚀","title":"Getting Started with Review","points":["Make review materials (summary cards, mind maps) <b>little by little from class onward</b>. Before the test, you'll only need to ‘use’ them, not ‘make’ them.","Put <b>understanding and context</b> before memorizing. Facts linked to ‘why?’ are hard to forget.","If starting is hard, begin with the <b>easiest, most fun part</b> to get into study mode.","After a review session, <b>reward</b> yourself! It keeps motivation going."],"quiz":[{"q":"Best time to make review materials?","o":["The day before the test","Little by little, right after classes all semester","After the test","All at once during vacation"],"a":1,"e":"Materials built up gradually greatly reduce exam-time pressure."}]},{"id":"5-2","emoji":"🚧","title":"Common Review Traps","points":["<b>The rereading trap</b>: reading many times makes it familiar, so you think you ‘know’ it (fluency illusion). Try recalling, and it's gone.","<b>The highlighter trap</b>: you don't think while coloring. Writing one line on ‘why it matters’ afterward is the real studying.","<b>The cramming trap</b>: you may pass the test, but most of it is gone a week later.","<b>Procrastination & distraction</b>: beat them by putting your phone away, starting a timer and doing the first 5 minutes."],"fact":{"title":"Research in one line","text":"Dunlosky and colleagues (2013) reviewed 10 common study techniques. They rated <b>practice testing (retrieval)</b> and <b>distributed practice (spacing)</b> as ‘high’ utility, and <b>rereading, highlighting and summarizing</b> as ‘low’."},"quiz":[{"q":"Which method did research rate ‘high’?","o":["Highlighting","Rereading over and over","Testing yourself + spacing study over time","Copying summaries"],"a":2,"e":"Retrieval practice and spaced repetition received the highest ratings."}]},{"id":"5-3","emoji":"📆","title":"Review Timetable","points":["<b>Weekly review</b>: spend 30 minutes at the weekend recalling this week's learning.","<b>Monthly review</b>: link a month's content on a single mind map.","During exam season, make a <b>priority list</b> (weakest units first) and <b>mix</b> subjects in your schedule.","Put <b>breaks in your timetable</b> too, like 10 minutes after every 50 minutes of study.","Meeting the same unit again at <b>1 → 3 → 7 → 14 days</b> makes memories last much longer."],"tip":"Enter your exam date and units into the ‘Exam Countdown Review Plan’ in the Toolbox to auto-create a spaced schedule.","toolLink":"exam","quiz":[{"q":"Which spaced review schedule is best?","o":["5 times in a row the day before the test","Again after 1 → 3 → 7 → 14 days","The same unit every day","Look once and done"],"a":1,"e":"The key is meeting it again at gradually longer intervals."}]},{"id":"5-4","emoji":"🃏","title":"Review Cards (Flashcards)","points":["<b>Question</b> on the front, <b>answer</b> on the back. Best for definitions, dates, formulas and vocabulary.","<b>Only one thing</b> per card. Whole paragraphs make cards less effective.","Before looking at the back, <b>always say the answer first</b>. That's the core of retrieval practice!","<b>Leitner box</b>: right → next box (less often), wrong → box 1 (more often). You'll see hard cards more often.","Carry them in your pocket for <b>mini reviews</b> on the bus or during breaks!"],"tip":"Make and review your own cards in the ‘Leitner Flashcards’ tool in the Toolbox.","toolLink":"cards","quiz":[{"q":"In a Leitner box, a card you got wrong…","o":["is thrown away","goes to the last box","goes back to box 1 so you see it more often","stays where it is"],"a":2,"e":"Seeing wrong cards more often is the core of the Leitner method."}]},{"id":"5-5","emoji":"🗺️","title":"Note Styles & Mind Maps","points":["<b>Standard</b>: title–headings–numbers. Good when order matters.","<b>Split-page (Cornell)</b>: notes on the right, questions/key words on the left, summary at the bottom. Test yourself with the left-side questions!","<b>Mind map</b>: central topic → branches (subtopics) → twigs (details). Shows how a whole unit connects.","<b>Flowchart</b>: perfect for processes and sequences (e.g. digestion, causes → effects of a historical event)!","The best part is <b>redrawing it without looking</b>. The blank spots are what to review."],"tip":"Organize one unit with the ‘Mind Map Maker’ in the Toolbox.","toolLink":"mindmap","quiz":[{"q":"Best format for ‘the water cycle’?","o":["Flowchart","Vocabulary list","Timeline","Highlighter"],"a":0,"e":"Content with steps and processes fits a flowchart."}]},{"id":"5-6","emoji":"🏰","title":"Mnemonic Toolkit","points":["<b>Acronyms</b>: make a word from first letters. e.g. rainbow colors ‘ROY G BIV’.","<b>Acrostics</b>: make a sentence from first letters. e.g. turn the planet order ‘MVEMJSUN’ into a funny sentence.","<b>Method of loci (memory palace)</b>: ‘place’ items one by one in familiar spots at home and walk through them to recall.","<b>Stories, rhythm, songs</b>: turn what you must learn into a silly story or song lyrics!","Note: mnemonics are great for <b>lists and sequences</b>, but they <b>can't replace understanding</b>."],"game":"loci","tip":"Make your own acronym with the ‘Mnemonic Maker’ in the Toolbox.","toolLink":"mnemonic","quiz":[{"q":"What's the principle of the ‘method of loci (memory palace)’?","o":["Reading aloud 100 times","Placing items in familiar locations to recall them","Collecting first letters only","Only drawing pictures"],"a":1,"e":"It uses the order of a place you know well to pull out memories."}]},{"id":"5-7","emoji":"📱","title":"Remembering with Technology","points":["<b>Spaced-repetition apps</b> (Anki, Quizlet, etc.) show cards again just as you're about to forget them.","For tricky content, <b>record question–answer pairs in your own voice</b> with a 3-second pause after each question. Answering before you hear it is retrieval practice.","Turn formulas or words you often miss into <b>sticky-note quizzes</b> on your door or mirror, and say them with the answer covered each time you pass.","But apps are just tools! Don't spend time decorating — <b>review right away</b>."],"quiz":[{"q":"The advantage of spaced-repetition apps?","o":["They show every card the same number of times daily","They show cards again just before you forget, for efficient memory","They take the test for you","They decorate your cards"],"a":1,"e":"Recalling just before you forget makes memory strongest."}]},{"id":"5-8","emoji":"🎯","title":"Know the Marking Criteria & Review Together","points":["Check the <b>rubric</b> and marks first. You need to know what earns points.","Learn question types and timing with <b>past papers</b>, and practice under timed, real-test conditions.","<b>Predict and write</b> likely questions yourself. Seeing through the examiner's eyes reveals the key points.","<b>Review groups</b>: quiz each other and take turns explaining. Set a time and agenda to avoid just chatting.","<b>Peer and self-assessment</b>: marking your own answers with the rubric makes weaknesses clear."],"quiz":[{"q":"How do you run a review group effectively?","o":["Meet and each look at your phone","Set time and agenda, quiz and explain to each other","One person only reads a summary aloud","Snacks and chatting"],"a":1,"e":"Asking and explaining to each other is the heart of group review."}]}]},{"id":6,"emoji":"📤","color":"#D63031","title":"Output Skills","sub":"Thinking · writing · presenting · tests","intro":"This is where you bring out and show what you know. Learn to organize your thinking into writing and speech, and to perform at your best in exams.","lessons":[{"id":"6-1","emoji":"🧩","title":"Building Thinking Power","points":["<b>Critical thinking</b>: claim → evidence → hidden assumptions → are there other explanations?","<b>Reflective thinking</b>: regularly ask ‘What went well? What should I change? How next time?’","<b>Creative thinking</b>: hold off judging and pour out lots of ideas (brainstorming); try unexpected connections (lateral thinking).","<b>Logical thinking</b>: link causes and effects step by step.","<b>Devil's advocate game</b>: pick a claim you agree with and build the 3 strongest counterarguments. Your thinking gets sharper."],"game":"critical","quiz":[{"q":"Which is NOT a critical-thinking question?","o":["What evidence supports this claim?","Are there hidden assumptions?","A famous person said it, so it must be right?","Is another explanation possible?"],"a":2,"e":"Judging by authority is the opposite of critical thinking."}]},{"id":"6-2","emoji":"✍️","title":"Writing: Decoding the Question & Building Arguments","points":["Read assignment questions through <b>four lenses</b>: <b>topic</b> (about what?) · <b>task verb</b> (analyse? compare? evaluate?) · <b>angle</b> (which aspect?) · <b>conditions</b> (limits like time, place, length).","<b>Outline</b> before writing! Mind map → paragraph plan → draft.","<b>One paragraph = one point</b>. Point → Evidence → Explain → Link.","In the conclusion, instead of new content, give a <b>clear final answer to the question</b>.","Before submitting, use a <b>revision checklist</b>: Did I answer the question? One point per paragraph? Evidence and sources? Spelling?"],"tip":"Try a real assignment question in the ‘Assignment Question Decoder’ in the Toolbox.","toolLink":"essay","game":"verbs","quiz":[{"q":"In ‘Evaluate the impact of 20th-century industrialization in Britain on city life’, what are the ‘conditions (limits)’?","o":["Evaluate","City life","20th-century Britain","Impact"],"a":2,"e":"Time and place (20th century, Britain) are the conditions. The task verb is ‘evaluate’; the angle is ‘impact on city life’."}]},{"id":"6-3","emoji":"🎤","title":"Presenting Well","points":["First decide who your <b>audience</b> is and what you want them to take away.","Structure: <b>opening</b> (hook + what you'll cover) → <b>body</b> (3 key points) → <b>closing</b> (summary + one-sentence message).","Keep slide text short, with pictures and key words. Don't read your slides.","Rehearse <b>out loud 3+ times</b> and time yourself. Recording and listening is even better.","Nerves are normal! Breathe slowly and memorize just your first sentence perfectly — the rest will follow."],"quiz":[{"q":"Most effective way to prepare a presentation?","o":["Fill the slides with your script","Practice only in your head","Rehearse out loud several times with a timer","Improvise on the day"],"a":2,"e":"Rehearsing out loud builds real performance skill."}]},{"id":"6-4","emoji":"📋","title":"Know Your Test Types","points":["Tests vary: essay, short answer, multiple choice, true/false, oral, practical, project assessment.","Some tests give questions <b>in advance</b>; others have questions you've <b>never seen</b>.","Reviewing steadily all semester greatly reduces exam stress.","Get used to the <b>format and question types</b> with past papers."],"quiz":[{"q":"Best way to learn the test format in advance?","o":["Listen to rumors from friends","Check past papers and the marking criteria","Find out on test day","Look at the textbook cover"],"a":1,"e":"Past papers and marking criteria are your map of the test."}]},{"id":"6-5","emoji":"🖊️","title":"Essay Exam Strategies","points":["Start by reading <b>the instructions carefully</b>. Miss a condition like ‘choose two’ and you'll lose marks on questions you know.","<b>Marks = a hint for answer length</b>. One line for a 10-mark question or a page for 2 marks costs you.","Do <b>easy questions first</b> to build confidence and save time.","For long questions, jot a <b>30-second outline</b> (3–4 key words) before writing.","<b>Divide your time</b> by marks, and leave the last 5 minutes for checking."],"game":"timecalc","quiz":[{"q":"In a 60-minute test worth 100 marks, how long for a 20-mark question? (excluding 5 min of checking)","o":["About 5 min","About 11 min","About 30 min","About 55 min"],"a":1,"e":"55 min × 20% = 11 min. Divide time by marks."}]},{"id":"6-6","emoji":"🔘","title":"Multiple-Choice & True/False Strategies","points":["<b>Think of the answer first</b>, before looking at the options. Then find it among them and you'll fall for fewer traps.","<b>Elimination</b>: cross out options that are clearly wrong first.","<b>Absolute words</b> like ‘always’, ‘never’ and ‘all’ often signal a wrong option (but not always!).","Circle negative questions like ‘Which is <b>NOT</b> correct?’","Mark questions you don't know, move on, and <b>come back at the end</b>. If there's no penalty, don't leave blanks."],"quiz":[{"q":"A good first step for a multiple-choice question?","o":["Read options first and pick the longest","Read only the question, think of the answer, then check options","Always pick C","Leave it blank if unsure"],"a":1,"e":"Thinking of the answer first makes you less swayed by tempting wrong options."}]},{"id":"6-7","emoji":"🗣️","title":"Oral & Practical Exams","points":["<b>Listen to the whole question</b>, and if it's unclear, it's fine to ask, ‘Could you repeat the question?’","Thinking for 2–3 seconds before answering is natural. You can say, ‘Let me think for a moment.’","Answer in the order <b>conclusion → reason → example</b>.","For practical exams, the best preparation is practicing many times <b>under real conditions</b>."],"quiz":[{"q":"What to do in an oral exam when a question is confusing?","o":["Talk at length about anything","Ask to clarify the question","Stay silent","Answer a different question"],"a":1,"e":"Understanding the question exactly is the start of a good answer."}]},{"id":"6-8","emoji":"🎒","title":"Exam-Day Checklist","points":["<b>Sleep well</b> the night before. Sleep raises scores more than last-minute cramming.","Check <b>time, place and supplies</b> (ID, admission ticket, pens, calculator, watch, water) the day before.","Eat a light breakfast and <b>arrive early</b> to stay calm.","Right before the test, skim only your <b>summary cards</b>, not new material.","After the start signal: read instructions → scan the whole paper → plan your time → easy questions first!"],"checklist":["Check exam time & place","Admission ticket & ID","2+ sets of writing tools","Watch (if allowed)","Calculator & other allowed tools","Water & a light snack","Summary cards","7+ hours of sleep"],"quiz":[{"q":"Best thing to do the night before a test?","o":["Stay up all night starting a new unit","Glance over summary cards and sleep well","Play games all night to relax","Drink three cups of coffee"],"a":1,"e":"Sleep is the best exam prep — it organizes your memories."}]},{"id":"6-9","emoji":"📈","title":"Growing from Results","points":["When results come, don't just look at the score — sort out <b>why you lost marks</b>.","4 types of mistakes: <b>① concept gap</b> ② <b>careless slip</b> (calculation, writing) ③ <b>misread question</b> ④ <b>ran out of time</b>.","Each type has its own cure: concept → re-understand & retrieve / slip → checking habit / misreading → underline conditions / time → practice time allocation.","Before the next test, redo your <b>mistake notebook</b>. Not repeating the same mistakes is the fastest way to raise your score."],"game":"errors","quiz":[{"q":"You read ‘Choose the one that is NOT correct’ as ‘correct’ and got it wrong. Which type?","o":["Concept gap","Misread question","Ran out of time","Bad luck"],"a":1,"e":"You misread the question's condition. The cure: underline conditions!"}]}]},{"id":7,"emoji":"🌿","color":"#00CEC9","title":"Recovery & Growth","sub":"Wellbeing · health · together","intro":"Everyone gets nervous. What matters is knowing how to handle it. Caring for your body and mind, and giving and receiving help, matter as much as study skills.","lessons":[{"id":"7-1","emoji":"💓","title":"What Is Exam Stress?","points":["<b>Body signals</b>: racing heart, sweaty hands, stomachache, headache, trouble sleeping.","<b>Mind signals</b>: worries that spiral, can't focus, irritability, ‘it's all going to go wrong’ thoughts.","<b>Some tension helps</b>. Too little and you're bored; too much and your mind goes blank.","Interpret a racing heart not as ‘I'm doomed’ but as <b>‘my body is getting ready to perform’</b>. Studies have found this reappraisal can raise test scores."],"fact":{"title":"Research in one line","text":"In several studies, students guided to see their stress reactions as ‘helpful energy’ performed better on tests (stress reappraisal)."},"quiz":[{"q":"A helpful thought when your heart races before a test?","o":["It's all over now","My body is getting ready to focus","I'm the only one this nervous","I should give up the test"],"a":1,"e":"Reframing tension as a ‘ready signal’ actually gives you strength."}]},{"id":"7-2","emoji":"🌡️","title":"Stress Thermometer","points":["Rate your stress from <b>1 to 10</b>. Putting a number on it makes it easier to handle.","<b>1–3</b>: as usual. <b>4–6</b>: more rest, exercise and talking. <b>7–10</b>: reduce your load and be sure to talk to a trusted adult.","The key is acting <b>before</b> the score climbs.","Split your worries into ‘things I can change / can't change’, and spend energy only on what you can change."],"tip":"Measure your stress temperature now in ‘Mood Check & Breathing’ in the Toolbox.","toolLink":"breath","quiz":[{"q":"If your stress score is 8?","o":["Endure it alone","Study even longer","Adjust your to-do list and talk to a trusted adult","Do nothing"],"a":2,"e":"A high score is a signal to ask for help."}]},{"id":"7-3","emoji":"💪","title":"Handling Pressure & Staying Healthy","points":["Stress is originally your body's way of preparing for danger. Some tension actually helps you focus.","Rest isn't a luxury — it's a <b>must</b>. Move your body every day, like 30 minutes of walking, swimming or cycling. Exercise lifts your mood and sends more oxygen to your brain.","For its weight, the brain uses a lot of energy. Instead of snacks and soda that burn out fast, fuel it steadily with <b>whole grains, fruit, vegetables and protein</b> — and drink enough water!","Teenagers usually need <b>8–10 hours</b> of sleep. Without enough, focus, judgment and memory all drop.","Focus on <b>realistic goals</b> rather than comparing yourself with others."],"fact":{"title":"Research in one line","text":"Sleep is when your brain presses ‘save’ on memories. Many studies show people who sleep after studying remember better than those who stay awake. An all-nighter is like switching off without saving."},"quiz":[{"q":"Closest to the recommended sleep for teenagers?","o":["4–5 hours","6 hours","8–10 hours","12+ hours"],"a":2,"e":"Teenagers are recommended about 8–10 hours of sleep a day."}]},{"id":"7-4","emoji":"🥗","title":"Healthy Study Habits","points":["<b>Sleep</b>: go to bed and get up at similar times every day. Screens off 1 hour before bed.","<b>Exercise</b>: even a short walk lifts mood and focus. Move between study sessions.","<b>Food</b>: don't skip breakfast, drink water often, and go easy on caffeine and energy drinks.","<b>People</b>: time with friends and family is essential for recovery too."],"quiz":[{"q":"A habit for sleeping well?","o":["Using your phone right up to bedtime","Catching up on sleep at weekends","Going to bed and getting up at similar times every day","Energy drinks late at night"],"a":2,"e":"Regular sleep times improve sleep quality."}]},{"id":"7-5","emoji":"☕","title":"Taking a Break (Time Out)","points":["You charge your phone before the battery dies, right? <b>Resting before you're exhausted</b> is the secret to studying for the long run.","Good breaks: a walk, stretching, music, looking out the window, chatting with a friend.","Careful: social media and short videos feel like rest, but your brain keeps working. 5 minutes easily becomes 50.","Once a week, plan <b>recharge time</b> when you put studying down completely."],"quiz":[{"q":"Which break really rests your brain?","o":["Watching short videos back to back","A walk or stretching","Scrolling a social feed","Doing another subject's workbook"],"a":1,"e":"Breaks that get you moving and away from screens help you recover."}]},{"id":"7-6","emoji":"🌬️","title":"Relaxation, Visualization & Positive Self-Talk","points":["<b>Long exhale</b>: breathing out longer than in switches your body into calm mode. e.g. in for 4 seconds, out for 6–8.","<b>Muscle relaxation</b>: tense your shoulders and fists for 5 seconds, then let go — repeat.","<b>Picture success</b>: imagine yourself walking into the exam room and calmly solving problems, in detail.","<b>Worry dump</b>: research shows writing all your worries on paper 10 minutes before a test frees up mental space.","<b>Change your self-talk</b>: ‘What if I fail?’ → ‘I'll solve them one by one, as I prepared’."],"fact":{"title":"Research in one line","text":"In a study by Ramirez & Beilock (2011), students who wrote about their test worries for about 10 minutes just before a test did better than those who didn't. It's thought that writing freed up the working-memory space worries were taking."},"toolLink":"breath","quiz":[{"q":"The key to a calming breathing technique?","o":["Breathe fast and short","Make the out-breath longer than the in-breath","Hold your breath","Breathe fast through your mouth only"],"a":1,"e":"A longer out-breath switches your body into rest mode."}]},{"id":"7-7","emoji":"🤝","title":"Growing Together","points":["Parents, teachers and friends aren't people who study for you — they're <b>coaches who help set the direction</b>.","Giving and receiving feedback in the order ‘what went well → what to fix → encouragement’ (the sandwich method) hurts less.","Find your best study time (morning or evening person) and share it with your family to reduce interruptions.","Asking about what you don't know isn't weakness — it's <b>the fastest learning strategy</b>."],"tip":"Once this week, ask your teacher: ‘I understood this part like this — is that right?’","quiz":[{"q":"The order of the ‘sandwich method’ for feedback?","o":["fix → fix → fix","what went well → what to fix → encouragement","encouragement → score → done","comparison → criticism → praise"],"a":1,"e":"Positive → specific improvement → encouragement makes feedback easier to accept."}]},{"id":"7-8","emoji":"🆘","title":"Asking for Help Takes Courage","points":["When it's too much to handle alone, talk to a <b>teacher, parent, friend or school counselor</b>. Asking for help isn't weakness — it's courage.","If you can't sleep, find it hard to eat, or don't feel like doing anything for 2 weeks or more, be sure to get help from an adult or professional.","In an emergency, call your local emergency number. Many countries have free youth helplines — ask your school counselor which one serves your area.","A test is just one scene in your life. You are worth far more than any score."],"quiz":[{"q":"If stress is too much to handle alone?","o":["Tell no one","Ask a trusted adult or counseling service for help","Study even more","It'll get better if I just endure it"],"a":1,"e":"Asking for help is the wisest and bravest choice."}]}]}],"labs":[{"id":"retrieval","emoji":"🧲","name":"Retrieval Practice","en":"Retrieval Practice","level":"High","what":"Practicing ‘pulling out’ information from memory with the book closed. Flashcards, blank-page recall, making your own questions and explaining to a friend are all retrieval.","why":"Each time you pull something out, the road to that memory gets wider. Experiments have repeatedly shown it lasts much longer than rereading.","how":["Close the book right after studying","Set a 3-minute timer and write everything you remember on a blank page","Open the book and fill in what you missed in another color","What you missed = top priority for your next review"],"demo":"braindump"},{"id":"spacing","emoji":"📅","name":"Spaced Practice","en":"Spaced Practice","level":"High","what":"Studying the same content several times with gaps in between, instead of all at once.","why":"Recalling something when you've half-forgotten it makes the memory stronger. Crammed material fades fast. Move the forgetting curve yourself!","how":["What you learned today → again after 1 day → 3 days → 7 days → 14 days","When reviewing, recall first instead of rereading","Auto-create a schedule with the exam countdown tool"],"demo":"curve"},{"id":"interleaving","emoji":"🔀","name":"Interleaving","en":"Interleaving","level":"Moderate","what":"Practicing a mix of problem types and units instead of one type in a row.","why":"When problems are mixed, you have to choose ‘which method fits this one?’ each time — just like real tests! It feels harder at first, and that's normal (a desirable difficulty).","how":["Alternate between 2–3 units instead of finishing one workbook unit","Identify the problem type first, then solve","Mix related topics rather than very different subjects"],"demo":"interleave"},{"id":"elaboration","emoji":"❓","name":"Elaborative Interrogation","en":"Elaboration","level":"Moderate","what":"Asking and answering ‘why?’ and ‘how?’ to connect new knowledge with what you already know.","why":"The more connections, the more roads lead to a memory. A memorized fact is a lonely island; a connected fact is a city on a road network.","how":["Pick one key concept","Why is it so? How does it work? How is it similar to or different from other things?","Connect it to your own experience","Check your answers against sources"],"demo":"why"},{"id":"concrete","emoji":"🍎","name":"Concrete Examples","en":"Concrete Examples","level":"Moderate","what":"Understanding abstract ideas through concrete examples. The key is finding what several examples have in common!","why":"Abstract ideas are hard to grasp, but examples create a picture in your mind.","how":["Read the definition","2 textbook examples + 1 of your own","What the examples share = the core of the concept"],"demo":"example"},{"id":"dual","emoji":"🖼️","name":"Dual Coding","en":"Dual Coding","level":"Supported","what":"Using words and pictures (diagrams, timelines, flowcharts, comics) together so information is stored along two routes.","why":"Verbal and visual memories help each other. If one route is blocked, you can retrieve through the other.","how":["Read a written explanation","Turn it into a picture/diagram (no need to be pretty!)","Explain using only the picture","Redraw the picture from only the explanation"],"demo":"draw"},{"id":"feynman","emoji":"👩‍🏫","name":"Learning by Teaching (Feynman Technique)","en":"Self-explanation","level":"Moderate","what":"Explain a concept in simple words as if to a child, find where you get stuck, and study that part again.","why":"To explain, you must organize your thinking. Where your explanation gets stuck is exactly what you don't know.","how":["Write the concept's name","Write an explanation in simple words (no jargon)","Mark where you got stuck → study it again","Make it simpler with an analogy"],"demo":"feynman"},{"id":"meta","emoji":"🪞","name":"Metacognition (Plan–Monitor–Evaluate)","en":"Metacognition","level":"High","what":"The ability to know ‘what I know and what I don't’. A cycle of planning before studying, checking along the way, and evaluating afterward.","why":"The UK Education Endowment Foundation (EEF) reports that metacognitive and self-regulated learning strategies add about 7–8 months of progress over a year on average. A low-cost, high-impact strategy.","how":["Plan: set goals, methods and time","Monitor: ask midway, ‘Do I understand this now?’","Evaluate: what worked? What next time?","Check your metacognition score with the ‘confidence’ choices in this app's quizzes!"],"demo":"calib"},{"id":"ifthen","emoji":"🔗","name":"If-Then Action Plans","en":"Implementation Intentions","level":"Supported","what":"Deciding in advance in the form ‘If [situation], then I will [action]’.","why":"Many studies show this raises follow-through far more than resolutions alone (Gollwitzer & Sheeran, 2006 meta-analysis). Deciding in advance means you need less willpower in the moment.","how":["If I get home from school and put my bag down → 10 vocabulary cards right away","If I feel like picking up my phone → drink a glass of water and restart the timer","WOOP: Wish → Outcome → Obstacle → Plan"],"demo":"woop"},{"id":"low","emoji":"⚠️","name":"Low-Utility Habits","en":"Low-utility habits","level":"Low","what":"Rereading, highlighting and simply copying summaries are the most common methods, yet they're rated low in effectiveness.","why":"They make familiarity feel like ‘knowing’ (fluency illusion). You don't need to drop them entirely, but always combine them with retrieval practice.","how":["Highlighter → after coloring, write 1 question in the margin","Rereading → recall first, before reading","Summarizing → write the summary with the book closed"],"demo":"rank"}],"diagAreas":[{"k":"plan","name":"Planning & time","ch":3,"tool":"week"},{"k":"focus","name":"Focus","ch":3,"tool":"timer"},{"k":"memory","name":"Memory & review","ch":5,"tool":"cards"},{"k":"info","name":"Reading & notes","ch":4,"tool":"cornell"},{"k":"exam","name":"Test strategy","ch":6,"tool":"exam"},{"k":"mind","name":"Wellbeing","ch":7,"tool":"breath"}],"diagQs":[["plan","I have a place (planner/app) to record tasks and deadlines",1],["plan","I tend to put off assignments until just before the deadline",-1],["plan","I make a weekly plan and check it at the weekend",1],["focus","I put my phone somewhere else when I study",1],["focus","I watch videos or social media while studying",-1],["focus","I can focus on one thing for a set amount of time",1],["memory","I practice closing the book and recalling what I remember",1],["memory","I tend to cram the day before a test",-1],["memory","I review what I learned again a few days later",1],["info","Before reading, I skim titles and headings for the big picture",1],["info","I rewrite my notes in my own words",1],["info","I highlight almost everything that looks important",-1],["exam","When I get a test, I check the instructions and marks first",1],["exam","I analyze why I got questions wrong and keep a mistake notebook",1],["exam","I practice timing for tests in advance",1],["mind","I often get so nervous before tests that my mind goes blank",-1],["mind","I get enough sleep (around 8 hours)",1],["mind","I have someone to talk to when things are hard",1]],"prefs":[{"k":"visual","name":"Pictures & diagrams","emoji":"👁️","tip":"Draw mind maps, flowcharts and timelines. Then go one step further and explain them out loud!"},{"k":"verbal","name":"Words & talk","emoji":"💬","tip":"You'll enjoy explaining, summarizing and discussing. Add practice turning things into diagrams too."},{"k":"logic","name":"Logic & patterns","emoji":"🧮","tip":"Cause→effect charts, comparison tables and sorting problem types suit you. Try story-style mnemonics as well."},{"k":"music","name":"Sound & rhythm","emoji":"🎵","tip":"Learn with rhythms and songs, or record and listen. But turn off music with lyrics while studying."},{"k":"body","name":"Body & hands","emoji":"🤸","tip":"Sort cards by hand, learn while walking, build models. Move briefly and often."},{"k":"social","name":"Together","emoji":"👥","tip":"Study groups, quizzing each other and teaching suit you. Make sure to keep solo retrieval time too!"}],"prefQs":[["visual","When I hear an explanation, I picture it in my head"],["visual","I'm good with directions and maps"],["verbal","I like reading and word games"],["verbal","I'm good at explaining things to others"],["logic","I'm good at spotting patterns and rules"],["logic","I like making ordered lists of things to do"],["music","Song lyrics and rhythms stick easily"],["music","I notice background sounds quickly"],["body","I like making and fixing things myself"],["body","It's hard for me to sit still for long"],["social","I enjoy solving problems with friends"],["social","Discussion helps me organize my thoughts"]],"badges":[{"id":"first","emoji":"🌱","name":"First Step","desc":"First lesson complete"},{"id":"diag","emoji":"🩺","name":"Self-Check","desc":"Study habit check complete"},{"id":"ch1","emoji":"🧠","name":"Brain Explorer","desc":"World 1 boss cleared"},{"id":"ch2","emoji":"🔥","name":"Mind Engine Ignited","desc":"World 2 boss cleared"},{"id":"ch3","emoji":"⏱️","name":"Time Master","desc":"World 3 boss cleared"},{"id":"ch4","emoji":"📥","name":"Info Hunter","desc":"World 4 boss cleared"},{"id":"ch5","emoji":"🔁","name":"Memory Master","desc":"World 5 boss cleared"},{"id":"ch6","emoji":"📤","name":"Game-Day Strategist","desc":"World 6 boss cleared"},{"id":"ch7","emoji":"🌿","name":"Mind Guardian","desc":"World 7 boss cleared"},{"id":"pomo1","emoji":"🍅","name":"First Tomato","desc":"1 focus timer session complete"},{"id":"pomo10","emoji":"🔥","name":"Focus Artisan","desc":"10 focus timer sessions"},{"id":"cards50","emoji":"🃏","name":"Card Master","desc":"50 flashcard reviews"},{"id":"labs","emoji":"🔬","name":"Lab Researcher","desc":"Explored 5 study lab methods"},{"id":"streak3","emoji":"📆","name":"3-Day Streak","desc":"Studied 3 days in a row"},{"id":"streak7","emoji":"🏅","name":"7-Day Streak","desc":"Studied 7 days in a row"},{"id":"perfect","emoji":"💯","name":"Perfect","desc":"Perfect score on a boss quiz"},{"id":"calib","emoji":"🎯","name":"Metacognition Pro","desc":"Calibration score of 80+"},{"id":"journal","emoji":"📓","name":"Reflector","desc":"3 learning journal entries"},{"id":"planner","emoji":"🗓️","name":"Planner","desc":"Created an exam review plan"},{"id":"all","emoji":"👑","name":"Study Legend","desc":"Cleared all 7 worlds"}],"levels":["🌱 Seedling Learner","🌿 Steady Explorer","🌳 Focus Trainee","⭐ Strategist","🚀 Self-Directed Runner","🏆 Study Master","👑 Learning Legend"],"missions":[{"id":"lesson","text":"Complete 1 lesson","xp":15},{"id":"pomo","text":"Finish 1 focus timer session","xp":20},{"id":"cards","text":"Review 10 flashcards","xp":20},{"id":"dump","text":"Do 1 brain dump (blank-page recall)","xp":15},{"id":"journal","text":"Write today's learning journal","xp":15},{"id":"lab","text":"Explore 1 study lab method","xp":10},{"id":"quiz","text":"Answer 3 quiz questions correctly","xp":15},{"id":"breath","text":"Breathe for 1 minute","xp":10}],"refs":[{"t":"Dunlosky, J. et al. (2013). Improving Students' Learning With Effective Learning Techniques. Psychological Science in the Public Interest, 14(1), 4–58.","u":"https://doi.org/10.1177/1529100612453266"},{"t":"APS press release: Which Study Strategies Make the Grade?","u":"https://www.psychologicalscience.org/news/releases/which-study-strategies-make-the-grade.html"},{"t":"The Learning Scientists — Six Strategies for Effective Learning (free materials)","u":"https://www.learningscientists.org/downloadable-materials"},{"t":"Pashler, H. et al. (2008). Learning Styles: Concepts and Evidence. PSPI, 9(3).","u":"https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1539-6053.2009.01038.x"},{"t":"Education Endowment Foundation — Metacognition and self-regulation (Teaching and Learning Toolkit)","u":"https://educationendowmentfoundation.org.uk/education-evidence/teaching-learning-toolkit/metacognition-and-self-regulation"},{"t":"Roediger, H. L. & Karpicke, J. D. (2006). Test-Enhanced Learning. Psychological Science, 17(3).","u":"https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1467-9280.2006.01693.x"},{"t":"Rohrer, D. & Taylor, K. (2007). The shuffling of mathematics problems improves learning. Instructional Science, 35.","u":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s11251-007-9015-8"},{"t":"Gollwitzer, P. M. & Sheeran, P. (2006). Implementation intentions and goal achievement: A meta-analysis.","u":"https://doi.org/10.1016/S0065-2601(06)38002-1"},{"t":"WOOP (Gabriele Oettingen) official site","u":"https://woopmylife.org/"},{"t":"Ramirez, G. & Beilock, S. L. (2011). Writing About Testing Worries Boosts Exam Performance in the Classroom. Science, 331.","u":"https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1199427"},{"t":"Sisk, V. F. et al. (2018). To What Extent and Under Which Circumstances Are Growth Mind-Sets Important to Academic Achievement? Psychological Science.","u":"https://doi.org/10.1177/0956797617739704"},{"t":"Korea Youth Counseling 1388 (Korea Youth Counseling & Welfare Institute)","u":"https://www.cyber1388.kr/"}]});
